From 8fab35ceb8c1f6250e77d6340f04ab9b1c1ec69a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matterward Labs <admin@matterwardlabs.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 19:07:28 +0530
Subject: witness the physical deletion of a profile directory

Chromium's profile deletion is fire-and-forget: NukeProfileFromDisk reports
nothing, and the kProfilesDeleted marker it leaves says a deletion was
accepted, never that one finished. A restore that adopts a target profile
needs the stronger fact, because publishing a target whose predecessor is
still on the disk is the failure the whole restart-recovery path exists to
refuse.

Add NukeProfileFromDiskWithResult, which reports true only after the exact
profile and derived cache paths are absent and their surviving parent
directory entries have been synchronized, and a read-only witness for a
deletion that already finished. Both are physical checks: absence is proved by
lstat and durability by fsync on the parent, so an answer survives a crash
rather than describing a page cache.

Also carry a dedicated deletion marker that names the exact path it belongs
to, so recovery can tell its own interrupted deletion from an unrelated one.
A malformed marker is conservative evidence, never authority to delete.

Specified by chromium/patches/0048-witness-physical-profile-deletion.md.

Taffy-Patch: 0048

